{"version":3,"kind":"Article","sha256":"01aaa64830940048717c357c2855780e24efd12142493ecd7b17bf25feff12c3","slug":"sections.mcs-projects.cse543-information-assurance-and-security.esper-cipher.esper-cipher","location":"/Sections/MCS_Projects/CSE543_Information_Assurance_and_Security/Esper_Cipher/Esper_Cipher.md","dependencies":[],"frontmatter":{"title":"CSE543 Information Assurance and Security - Esper Cipher","content_includes_title":false,"authors":[{"nameParsed":{"literal":"Mark Khusid","given":"Mark","family":"Khusid"},"name":"Mark Khusid","id":"contributors-myst-generated-uid-0"}],"github":"https://github.com/markkhusid/MKDynamics_Website_V2","settings":{"output_matplotlib_strings":"remove"},"numbering":{"title":{"offset":3}},"source_url":"https://github.com/markkhusid/MKDynamics_Website_V2/blob/main/Sections/MCS_Projects/CSE543_Information_Assurance_and_Security/Esper_Cipher/Esper_Cipher.md","edit_url":"https://github.com/markkhusid/MKDynamics_Website_V2/edit/main/Sections/MCS_Projects/CSE543_Information_Assurance_and_Security/Esper_Cipher/Esper_Cipher.md","exports":[{"format":"md","filename":"Esper_Cipher.md","url":"/build/Esper_Cipher-8880d8e15f5805142035c9cae3159c8f.md"}]},"mdast":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"block","children":[{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Created with Grok Build","position":{"start":{"line":3,"column":1},"end":{"line":3,"column":1}},"key":"qiSW8fR2FZ"}],"key":"TrvntMfZFk"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Esper, as used in this course, is a byte cipher with two steps. Each byte is rotated left by a small bit count, then exclusive-ored with a byte from a repeating key. Decryption exclusive-ors the same key byte and rotates right by the same count. Grokipedia has no article under this course’s name for the cipher. The two operations are the ","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"KE5OiSxHjg"},{"type":"link","url":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Circular_shift","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"circular shift","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"vYPlQK50bA"}],"urlSource":"https://grokipedia.com/page/Circular_shift","key":"GESROuuNNg"},{"type":"text","value":" and the ","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"KZqH26SBPQ"},{"type":"link","url":"https://grokipedia.com/page/XOR_cipher","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"XOR cipher","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"XTzMTsIvYO"}],"urlSource":"https://grokipedia.com/page/XOR_cipher","key":"Q2v9rCPhIf"},{"type":"text","value":". A repeating XOR key is a short keystream. The rotation only scrambles which bits meet which key bits. It does not add a new secret.","position":{"start":{"line":5,"column":1},"end":{"line":5,"column":1}},"key":"qvBypxnNFK"}],"key":"GpsWKsKMR8"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"The code decrypted a course ciphertext with a course key. The example below uses a new seven-byte key and a new sentence.","position":{"start":{"line":7,"column":1},"end":{"line":7,"column":1}},"key":"LOwyBIp6Pm"}],"key":"JninhMp5Ns"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Rotate, then exclusive-or","position":{"start":{"line":9,"column":1},"end":{"line":9,"column":1}},"key":"recFgAW9oO"}],"identifier":"rotate-then-exclusive-or","label":"Rotate, then exclusive-or","html_id":"rotate-then-exclusive-or","implicit":true,"key":"nXkGGuiYed"},{"type":"code","lang":"python","value":"def rotl(byte, count):\n    count = count & 7\n    byte = byte & 0xFF\n    if count == 0:\n        return byte\n    high = (byte << count) & 0xFF\n    low = byte >> (8 - count)\n    return high | low\n\ndef rotr(byte, count):\n    count = count & 7\n    if count == 0:\n        return byte & 0xFF\n    return rotl(byte, 8 - count)\n\ndef encrypt(data, key, count):\n    out = bytearray()\n    for index, byte in enumerate(data):\n        mixed = rotl(byte, count) ^ key[index % len(key)]\n        out.append(mixed)\n    return bytes(out)\n\ndef decrypt(data, key, count):\n    out = bytearray()\n    for index, byte in enumerate(data):\n        mixed = byte ^ key[index % len(key)]\n        out.append(rotr(mixed, count))\n    return bytes(out)","position":{"start":{"line":11,"column":1},"end":{"line":40,"column":1}},"key":"lP0j9VMayl"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"With key ","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"synpNkcPqZ"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Bright7","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"FKAKC1OHJz"},{"type":"text","value":" and a rotate of 3, ","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"HQl88QN251"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"Public demo only","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"MCH4TeuobS"},{"type":"text","value":" encrypts to the hex line ","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"R1PeuOg6tH"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"c0d97a04236f366159021c690f4421b9","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"kcdO30pzQC"},{"type":"text","value":". Decrypting with the same key and count returns the sentence.","position":{"start":{"line":42,"column":1},"end":{"line":42,"column":1}},"key":"W4Q6WtQASM"}],"key":"U5xe2PC9A4"},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Why the order matters","position":{"start":{"line":44,"column":1},"end":{"line":44,"column":1}},"key":"xhPxloxd2w"}],"identifier":"why-the-order-matters","label":"Why the order matters","html_id":"why-the-order-matters","implicit":true,"key":"u4j31WeeIY"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Exclusive-or is its own inverse, and a right rotation undoes a left rotation. Swap the order, or use a different count, and the bytes stay scrambled. That is the whole cipher: two invertible steps, applied in a fixed order.","position":{"start":{"line":46,"column":1},"end":{"line":46,"column":1}},"key":"ydHmsTqIxr"}],"key":"NLkj6FuPXg"}],"key":"LPKKfXm15e"}],"key":"s8Lk9TnKoi"},"references":{"cite":{"order":[],"data":{}}},"footer":{"navigation":{"prev":{"title":"CSE543 Information Assurance and Security - Caesar Cipher","url":"/sections/mcs-projects/cse543-information-assurance-and-security/caesar-cipher/caesar-cipher","group":"Master's in Computer Science (Cybersecurity) Projects"},"next":{"title":"CSE543 Information Assurance and Security - Finding Crashes","url":"/sections/mcs-projects/cse543-information-assurance-and-security/finding-crashes/finding-crashes","group":"Master's in Computer Science (Cybersecurity) Projects"}}},"domain":"https://www.mkdynamics.net"}